  "analyzed_vuln": {
    "function": "UA_WorkQueue_manuallyProcessDelayed",
    "file": "open62541/src/ua_workqueue.c",
    "description": "The delayed-queue drain uses SIMPLEQ_FOREACH_SAFE with a cached successor (`dc_tmp`) but removes `SIMPLEQ_HEAD` on every iteration. A callback may legally enqueue new delayed callbacks at the queue head. After such mutation, the cached iterator node and actual head differ: the next iteration removes the newly inserted head but executes and frees the cached node, leaving that freed node linked as the queue head. A subsequent drain reads the dangling node at line 251, causing the heap-use-after-free. The session cleanup callback is a concrete trigger because it can enqueue subscription/monitored-item delayed-free callbacks while the work queue is being drained.",
    "conditions": "A binary OPC UA message sequence must create session-owned subscription/monitored-item state and arrange multiple delayed callbacks, including removal of that session. During `UA_Server_run_shutdown`, draining the session's embedded cleanup callback calls `UA_Session_deleteMembersCleanup`, which prepends additional delayed-free callbacks for subscriptions/monitored items. With a previously cached successor in the queue, the iterator/head-removal mismatch leaves a freed callback linked. The harness then calls `UA_Server_delete`, which invokes `UA_WorkQueue_cleanup` a second time and dereferences the stale queue entry. The attacker-controlled HEL/OPN/MSG service sequence in the PoV establishes this state; the empty reported input_length is inconsistent with the embedded PoC, which contains the actual triggering bytes."
  },
